WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.576
Last night, I downloaded GTA 6.

00:00:04.076 --> 00:00:07.196
On a 1981 IBM PC.

00:00:07.696 --> 00:00:10.696
It took 368 days.

00:00:11.696 --> 00:00:13.472
Here's the strange part.

00:00:13.972 --> 00:00:15.988
I only started yesterday.

00:00:16.488 --> 00:00:18.456
Let's talk about this computer.

00:00:18.956 --> 00:00:20.420
1981.

00:00:20.920 --> 00:00:24.376
Four point seven seven megahertz.

00:00:24.876 --> 00:00:28.452
Six hundred and forty kilobytes of memory.

00:00:28.952 --> 00:00:31.808
No Wi-Fi. No internet.

00:00:32.308 --> 00:00:34.924
Honestly… no colors.

00:00:35.424 --> 00:00:39.600
GTA 6 is two hundred and twelve gigabytes.

00:00:40.100 --> 00:00:44.029
That is three hundred and forty-seven thousand times more

00:00:44.029 --> 00:00:47.084
than this computer can remember at once.

00:00:47.584 --> 00:00:51.208
This computer can hold the game's first icon.

00:00:51.708 --> 00:00:53.676
Not the icon file.

00:00:54.176 --> 00:00:56.696
The idea of the icon.

00:00:57.196 --> 00:01:00.268
Step one: get online.

00:01:00.768 --> 00:01:03.672
I plugged in a 56k modem.

00:01:04.172 --> 00:01:06.356
Seven kilobytes per second.

00:01:06.856 --> 00:01:11.461
Two hundred and twelve gigabytes at seven kilobytes a second…

00:01:11.461 --> 00:01:14.224
is three hundred and sixty-eight days.

00:01:14.724 --> 00:01:16.332
If nothing interrupts.

00:01:17.332 --> 00:01:19.468
Nothing ever interrupts.

00:01:19.968 --> 00:01:24.672
Day one: zero point zero zero zero four percent.

00:01:25.172 --> 00:01:29.804
Day two: zero point zero zero zero four percent.

00:01:30.304 --> 00:01:33.760
This is where you'd normally say… worth it.

00:01:34.260 --> 00:01:36.876
Step two: install.

00:01:37.376 --> 00:01:42.171
The game ships on six hundred and seventeen thousand,

00:01:42.171 --> 00:01:45.368
four hundred and ninety-five floppy disks.

00:01:45.868 --> 00:01:49.204
Stacked up, that's taller than the Burj Khalifa.

00:01:49.704 --> 00:01:51.480
Insert disk one.

00:01:51.980 --> 00:01:54.332
Insert disk two.

00:01:55.332 --> 00:01:57.108
I stopped at disk three.

00:01:57.608 --> 00:02:02.696
The floppy drive heard the word 'billion'… and retired.

00:02:03.196 --> 00:02:05.389
Step three: the game phones home

00:02:05.389 --> 00:02:07.948
to check if my copy is real.

00:02:08.448 --> 00:02:11.400
The server asks for my account.

00:02:11.900 --> 00:02:15.356
My computer asks what an account is.

00:02:15.856 --> 00:02:18.688
They negotiate for forty minutes.

00:02:19.188 --> 00:02:21.132
The download restarts.

00:02:21.632 --> 00:02:24.368
Day three hundred and sixty-nine.

00:02:24.868 --> 00:02:27.700
Step four: I play it.

00:02:28.200 --> 00:02:29.880
The screen turns on.

00:02:30.380 --> 00:02:32.228
One pixel appears.

00:02:33.228 --> 00:02:34.524
It's Jason.

00:02:35.024 --> 00:02:37.688
Another pixel flickers next to him.

00:02:38.188 --> 00:02:39.364
That's Lucia.

00:02:39.864 --> 00:02:43.026
Then the fan reaches a pitch only dogs can hear…

00:02:43.026 --> 00:02:47.136
and the screen says the most honest thing this computer has ever said:

00:02:48.136 --> 00:02:51.304
Grand Theft Auto VI — coming soon.

00:02:51.804 --> 00:02:54.468
Thirteen years of waiting.

00:02:54.968 --> 00:02:59.600
And somehow… my computer waited harder than me.

00:03:00.100 --> 00:03:03.679
So — what's the oldest machine you've ever tried

00:03:03.679 --> 00:03:05.668
to run something modern on?

00:03:06.168 --> 00:03:08.448
I need to know I'm not alone.

